sound: a node that is absent right now is not a stale mapping

The MPC's sampling input did not exist in the host graph on a cold start.
Not late, not silent - absent: no :mic node at all, so the launcher's
bridge had nothing to link the sampler sink into and gave up, and every
"route host audio into the sampler" attempt was aimed at a node that was
never created.

The cause is in sound_manager::generate_mapping(). A saved mapping that
names a specific host node is resolved with find_node(); when the name
does not resolve the entry was not skipped, it was DELETED from the
configuration - collected into node_to_remove and passed to
internal_config_remove_sound_io_connection_node(). This machine's saved
configuration maps :mic to o:MPC-Sampler-Input, the null sink the
launcher publishes, and on a cold start that sink does not exist yet. So
the mapping was destroyed on the first pass, the microphone was left with
no mapping at all, update_osd_streams() therefore never called
sound_stream_source_open() for it, and no capture node was created.
Nothing recovered when the sink appeared two seconds later, because the
entry it would have bound to was gone.

Measured on this workstation, all three with the emulator killed and the
sink unloaded first so nothing was pre-existing. Before: :mic still
absent three and a half minutes in, with the sink up and waiting. With
the sink created BEFORE launch, so the name resolved on the first pass:
:mic up in 1.0 s. After this change, sink created by the launcher as
usual, after the machine: :mic up in 1.55 s, and the bridge linked
mpc_sampler_in's monitor into it without help.

Also settled what the DSP had nothing to do with it. The previous
explanation was that MAME opens a capture node when the machine first
READS its input stream, and patch 0053 had the sound device read and
discard both input channels while idle to force it open. That was wrong:
a binary carrying that read shows the same empty graph in the same cold
start. The read never created anything, and it is gone.

Host nodes come and go - an interface plugged in late, a headset that
connects, a virtual sink published by a companion script - and absence on
one pass is not consent to forget where the user pointed the device.
Skipping the unresolved name leaves the entry in the configuration and
the next generation binds it, which is also what makes the recovery above
work. Removal stays in the sound menu, where it was asked for.
